msgid "For example, in the above graph we see an average airspeed of 36.5, and we would like to increase that by 2 to 38.5. The old ratio was 2.0. So we can calculate the new ratio as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/calibrating-an-airspeed-sensor.rst:161
#: b20a0b93dc2c4c1082aa98f2720ea09a
msgid "After adjusting the :ref:`ARSPD_RATIO <ARSPD_RATIO>` do another flight and check that the airspeed is now well calibrated. Don't forget the pre-flight checks!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/calibrating-an-airspeed-sensor.rst:165
#: 1dbcbead720840ddbc1ca57ecdc9ffaf
msgid "Note about higher altitudes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/calibrating-an-airspeed-sensor.rst:167
#: b10563e4a26844c5977924decc8eb3ff
msgid "At higher altitudes you expect the true airspeed and apparent airspeed to start to diverge. The above calibration procedure assumes true and apparent airspeed are the same. If you are flying at altitudes of 2000 meters or more above sea level then you should take account of the difference, and aim for your apparent airspeed to be below the average ground speed by the ratio of true airspeed to apparent airspeed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/calibrating-an-airspeed-sensor.rst:174
#: 616a9cfb24144182925d5ac46db54f07
msgid "At an altitude of 2000 meters above sea level the difference between apparent and true airspeed is around 10%, so you would expect your average airspeed to be below the average ground speed by about 10%. At 4000 meters above sea level this rises to around 20%. At 5000 meters it is around 30%. Beyond that please consult an aircraft navigation table."
msgstr ""
